<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/intermediate/sql-for-analysts">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    SQL is <strong>the analyst's primary interface to data</strong> — declarative (you describe
    the result, the engine finds the plan) and everywhere: warehouses, lakes, spreadsheets' back
    ends. Analyst-level SQL goes beyond SELECT: joins define your population, GROUP BY defines
    your granularity, and window functions unlock the questions that made older SQL painful.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four techniques that cover most analyst work" widget="equationStep" config={sqlCfg} />

  <h2>SQL habits that prevent wrong answers</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Always know your grain:</strong> what does one row represent after each step? A join that duplicates orders silently double-counts revenue.</li>
    <li><strong>NULLs are not zero:</strong> AVG ignores NULLs; "no sale" and "sale of 0" differ. COALESCE thoughtfully — and check whether NULLs changed your denominators.</li>
    <li><strong>Filter before you join where possible,</strong> and index the join keys — correctness first, but performance is an analyst skill too (nobody reruns a 40-minute query often).</li>
    <li><strong>Sanity-check totals against a known source</strong> — finance's numbers, the product dashboard's counts. Discrepancies are usually definitional (which table is "truth" for users?) and worth resolving explicitly.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> PostgreSQL manual; Mode SQL Tutorial; Kimball, The Data Warehouse Toolkit.</p>
</TopicPage>
